/-!
# Level-one automorphic forms for classical groups: target signatures

The roadmap is `README.md`; this file is not the roadmap. It states the roadmap's headline
theorems that are statable against the pinned Mathlib and Tau Ceti, each with `sorry`, together
with the few simple definitions they need.

Stated here:

* the spinor obstruction on characteristic polynomials of integral special orthogonal elements
  (README §4.7; Chenevier–Taïbi, Proposition 3.7): `spinor_charpoly_obstruction`;
* the isometry-invariant alternating forms of the Leech lattice (README §8.5; Chenevier–Taïbi,
  *Siegel modular forms of weight 13 and the Leech lattice*, formula (1) and Proposition 1.4):
  `finrank_invariantAlternatingForms_of_rootless`;
* the vanishing of invariant alternating forms on a lattice spanned by its roots (README §8.5;
  the same paper, Proposition 4.1): `invariantAlternatingForms_eq_bot_of_roots_span`;
* the nonvanishing of the harmonic Leech theta series in genera 8, 12, 16 and 24, as the
  nonvanishing of one of its Fourier coefficients (README §8.6; the same paper, Theorem 2):
  `exists_thetaCoefficient_ne_zero_of_rootless`.

Not statable at the pins, with the missing carrier:

* the dimension formula for `S_k(Sp_{2g}(ℤ))` for `g ≤ 8` and `k_g > g` (README §7.3), the
  twenty-nine nonscalar and thirty-two scalar eigenparameter rows in weight at most 13
  (§§7.8–7.9), the four weight-13 forms (§7.10), the Eichler basis theorem in weights 4, 8, 12
  (§8.1), the harmonic theta realizations and the alternating theta isomorphism (§§8.8, 8.10), and
  the stable dimensions, stable Siegel operator and stable theta isomorphism (§§8.12–8.14): Siegel
  modular forms of genus `g ≥ 2` for `Sp_{2g}(ℤ)`, their cusp forms, the Siegel operator and the
  genus-`g` lattice theta map are absent from Mathlib and Tau Ceti;
* the classifications of level-one algebraic cuspidal representations of `PGL_m` over `ℚ` in
  motivic weight at most 22, 23 and 24 and the conditional restriction under the generalized
  Riemann hypothesis (§§5.3–5.10), Miller's vanishing (§2.19), the repeated-weight `PGL₄` vanishing
  (§2.21) and the Stark–Odlyzko cone inequality (§2.7): cuspidal automorphic representations of
  `GL_m` over the adèles and their Rankin–Selberg `L`-functions are absent;
* the masses of torsion classes of `SO_d` (`d ≤ 17`) and `Sp_{2n}` (`n ≤ 8`) (§§4.13–4.18): the
  elliptic part of Arthur's `L²`-Lefschetz trace formula and its orbital integrals are absent;
* the exact value `±n_g e_g` of the `Q_g`-Fourier coefficient of the Leech theta series (§8.6):
  the Leech lattice itself is `TauCeti.leechLattice` at the pin, but the subgroup `2¹²M₂₄` of its
  isometries generated by Golay sign changes and the Mathieu permutations, the oriented orbit-sum
  forms `ω_g` it normalizes, and the sublattices `Q_g` are absent.

The Leech statements below are made for every positive definite even unimodular lattice of rank 24
without roots, which by the uniqueness of the rootless class is the Leech lattice; at the pin
`TauCeti.leechLattice` satisfies all of their hypotheses (`TauCeti.isEven_leechLattice`,
`TauCeti.isUnimodular_leechLattice`, `TauCeti.isPosDef_leechLattice`,
`TauCeti.vectorsOfNorm_two_leechLattice`).
-/

@[expose] public section

open Polynomial

namespace TauCetiRoadmap.LevelOneAutomorphicFormsForClassicalGroups

/-! ## Layer 4: geometric torsion classes and masses -/

/-- An integral quadratic form `x ↦ xᵀ c x` on `ℤⁿ`, given by an integer matrix `c`, is
*regular* when its polar Gram matrix `c + cᵀ` has determinant `±1` (even rank, the polar form is
a perfect pairing) or `±2` (odd rank, unit half-discriminant). This is Chenevier–Taïbi's notion
of a regular quadratic form over `ℤ` (CT20 §3.2), specialized to free `ℤ`-modules. -/
def IsRegularIntegralForm {n : ℕ} (c : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℤ) : Prop :=
  (Even n ∧ |(c + c.transpose).det| = 1) ∨ (Odd n ∧ |(c + c.transpose).det| = 2)

/-- The matrices of determinant one over a commutative ring `R` preserving the quadratic form
`x ↦ xᵀ c x` base changed to `R`. For `R` an integral domain in which `2 ≠ 0`, such as `ℤ_[p]`,
and `c` regular, these are the `R`-points of the special orthogonal group scheme of the form. -/
def specialOrthogonalPoints {n : ℕ} (c : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℤ) (R : Type*) [CommRing R] :
    Set (Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) R) :=
  {γ | γ.det = 1 ∧ ∀ x : Fin n → R,
      (c.map (Int.cast : ℤ → R)).toQuadraticForm' (γ.mulVec x) =
        (c.map (Int.cast : ℤ → R)).toQuadraticForm' x}

/-- **The spinor obstruction** (README §4.7; Chenevier–Taïbi, *Discrete series multiplicities
for classical groups over ℤ and level 1 algebraic cusp forms*, Proposition 3.7). Let `q` be a
regular quadratic form on `ℤⁿ` and `P = (X - 1)^a (X + 1)^(2b) Q` with `Q(1) Q(-1) ≠ 0`. If for
every prime `p` some element of `SO(q)(ℤ_p)` has characteristic polynomial `P`, then `|Q(-1)|` is
the square of an integer when `b = 0`, and `|Q(1)|` is the square of an integer when `a = 0`. -/
theorem spinor_charpoly_obstruction {n : ℕ} (c : Matrix (Fin n) (Fin n) ℤ)
    (hc : IsRegularIntegralForm c) (a b : ℕ) (Q : ℚ[X]) (hQ : Q.eval 1 * Q.eval (-1) ≠ 0)
    (hloc : ∀ (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime], ∃ γ ∈ specialOrthogonalPoints c ℤ_[p],
      γ.charpoly.map (PadicInt.Coe.ringHom (p := p)) =
        ((X - 1) ^ a * (X + 1) ^ (2 * b) * Q).map (algebraMap ℚ ℚ_[p])) :
    (b = 0 → ∃ m : ℤ, |Q.eval (-1)| = (m : ℚ) ^ 2) ∧
      (a = 0 → ∃ m : ℤ, |Q.eval 1| = (m : ℚ) ^ 2) := by
  sorry

/-! ## Layer 8: harmonic theta realizations and the stable range -/

open TauCeti

/-- The alternating `g`-forms `ω : Vᵍ → ℚ` on the rational space of an integral lattice `L`
that are invariant under every isometry of `L`: `ω(e v₁, …, e v_g) = ω(v₁, …, v_g)`
(README §8.5). Through the lattice's form these are the `O(L)`-invariants of `⋀^g (L ⊗ ℚ)`. -/
def invariantAlternatingForms {V : Type*} [AddCommGroup V] [Module ℚ V]
    (L : IntegralLattice V) (g : ℕ) : Submodule ℚ (V [⋀^Fin g]→ₗ[ℚ] ℚ) where
  carrier := {ω | ∀ (e : IntegralLattice.Isometry L L) (v : Fin g → V),
    ω (fun i => e (v i)) = ω v}
  add_mem' {ω ω'} hω hω' e v := by
    change ω (fun i => e (v i)) + ω' (fun i => e (v i)) = ω v + ω' v
    rw [hω e v, hω' e v]
  zero_mem' _ _ := rfl
  smul_mem' r ω hω e v := by
    change r • ω (fun i => e (v i)) = r • ω v
    rw [hω e v]

/-- The Fourier coefficient at the matrix `T` of the theta series
`Σ_{x ∈ Lᵍ} ω(x) exp(πi tr(Gram(x) τ))` attached to an alternating `g`-form `ω`: the sum of
`ω(x)` over the `g`-tuples `x` of lattice vectors with Gram matrix `T` (README §8.6). For a
positive definite lattice the summation set is finite. -/
noncomputable def thetaCoefficient {V : Type*} [AddCommGroup V] [Module ℚ V]
    (L : IntegralLattice V) {g : ℕ} (ω : V [⋀^Fin g]→ₗ[ℚ] ℚ)
    (T : Matrix (Fin g) (Fin g) ℚ) : ℚ :=
  ∑ᶠ (x : Fin g → L) (_ : Matrix.of (fun i j => L.form (x i) (x j)) = T),
    ω (fun i => (x i : V))

/-- **Invariant alternating forms of the Leech lattice** (README §8.5; Chenevier–Taïbi,
*Siegel modular forms of weight 13 and the Leech lattice*, formula (1) and Proposition 1.4).
For a positive definite even unimodular lattice of rank 24 without roots, that is the Leech
lattice, the isometry-invariant alternating `g`-forms form a line for `g ∈ {0, 8, 12, 16, 24}`
and vanish for every other `g`. -/
theorem finrank_invariantAlternatingForms_of_rootless {V : Type*} [AddCommGroup V]
    [Module ℚ V] (L : IntegralLattice V) (hev : L.IsEven) (huni : L.IsUnimodular)
    (hpos : L.IsPosDef) (hrank : Module.finrank ℚ V = 24)
    (hroot : ∀ x : L, L.norm (x : V) ≠ 2) (g : ℕ) :
    Module.finrank ℚ (invariantAlternatingForms L g) =
      if g ∈ ({0, 8, 12, 16, 24} : Finset ℕ) then 1 else 0 := by
  sorry

/-- **Lattices spanned by their roots carry no invariant alternating forms** (README §8.5;
Chenevier–Taïbi, *Siegel modular forms of weight 13 and the Leech lattice*, Proposition 4.1).
If the vectors of norm 2 of an integral lattice `L` span its rational space, then for every
`g ≥ 1` the only isometry-invariant alternating `g`-form is zero. This applies to the 23 Niemeier
lattices with roots. -/
theorem invariantAlternatingForms_eq_bot_of_roots_span {V : Type*} [AddCommGroup V]
    [Module ℚ V] (L : IntegralLattice V)
    (hspan : Submodule.span ℚ ((fun x : L => (x : V)) '' L.vectorsOfNorm 2) = ⊤)
    {g : ℕ} (hg : 0 < g) :
    invariantAlternatingForms L g = ⊥ := by
  sorry

/-- **The harmonic Leech theta series are nonzero** (README §8.6; Chenevier–Taïbi, *Siegel
modular forms of weight 13 and the Leech lattice*, Theorem 2). For the Leech lattice and
`g ∈ {8, 12, 16, 24}`, every nonzero isometry-invariant alternating `g`-form `ω` has a nonzero
theta Fourier coefficient; hence the weight-13 Siegel theta series `Σ_{x ∈ Lᵍ} ω(x) q^{Gram(x)/2}`
is nonzero. -/
theorem exists_thetaCoefficient_ne_zero_of_rootless {V : Type*} [AddCommGroup V]
    [Module ℚ V] (L : IntegralLattice V) (hev : L.IsEven) (huni : L.IsUnimodular)
    (hpos : L.IsPosDef) (hrank : Module.finrank ℚ V = 24)
    (hroot : ∀ x : L, L.norm (x : V) ≠ 2) {g : ℕ} (hg : g ∈ ({8, 12, 16, 24} : Finset ℕ))
    {ω : V [⋀^Fin g]→ₗ[ℚ] ℚ} (hω : ω ∈ invariantAlternatingForms L g) (hω0 : ω ≠ 0) :
    ∃ T : Matrix (Fin g) (Fin g) ℚ, thetaCoefficient L ω T ≠ 0 := by
  sorry

end TauCetiRoadmap.LevelOneAutomorphicFormsForClassicalGroups
